{"causes": ["The ongoing political fragmentation and internecine warfare of the Spring and Autumn period necessitated strong military leaders and administrators like Shuliang He.", "His noble birth within the Kong clan in the State of Lu provided him with opportunities for education and advancement in both civil and military spheres.", "The prevailing cultural ideals of the Zhou dynasty emphasized a blend of scholarly knowledge and martial skill for aristocratic men, which Shuliang He embodied."], "effects": ["Shuliang He's lineage and social standing provided the family environment into which Confucius was born and raised, impacting his early life and education.", "His military actions, such as saving Lu forces at Bi, contributed to the stability and defense of the State of Lu during a period of intense conflict.", "His reputation as a strong and principled official indirectly set a moral precedent that may have influenced his son's later ethical philosophy."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "shuliang-he", "sourceName": "Shuliang He", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "siege-of-bi", "targetName": "Siege of Bi", "context": "Shuliang He famously held open the portcullis during the Siege of Bi in 563 BCE, saving Lu forces."}, {"sourceSlug": "shuliang-he", "sourceName": "Shuliang He", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "state-of-lu", "targetName": "State of Lu", "context": "Shuliang He served as a scholar-official and military officer within the State of Lu during his lifetime."}, {"sourceSlug": "spring-and-autumn-period", "sourceName": "Spring and Autumn Period", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "shuliang-he", "targetName": "Shuliang He", "context": "Shuliang He lived and served as a military figure and official during the Spring and Autumn Period (771–476 BCE) in ancient China."}, {"sourceSlug": "confucius", "sourceName": "Confucius", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "shuliang-he", "targetName": "Shuliang He", "context": "Confucius (Kong Qiu) was the son of Shuliang He, inheriting his noble lineage though his father died when he was young."}, {"sourceSlug": "bo-xia", "sourceName": "Bo Xia", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "shuliang-he", "targetName": "Shuliang He", "context": "Bo Xia was the father of Shuliang He, establishing the family's presence in the Kong clan of Lu."}], "places": [{"name": "Qu Fu, Lu", "role": "Capital of Lu"}, {"name": "Zou, Lu", "role": "Birthplace/Residence"}, {"name": "Bi, Lu", "role": "Site of Heroic Act"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Shuliang He", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "shuliang-he"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q847621", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuliang_He"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Shuliang He's historical importance stems primarily from being the father of Confucius, whose philosophical system profoundly shaped East Asia, and secondarily from his own notable career as a military officer and administrator within the State of Lu during a critical transitional period.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:22:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36611350732", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Kong He, also known as Shuliang He, was a scholar-official and military officer of the State of Lu. He was the son of the Lu political figure Bo Xia and the father of Kong Pi and Confucius.", "newValue": "Shuliang He (died 547 BCE), also known by his courtesy name Kong He, was a distinguished scholar-official and formidable military officer during the tumultuous Spring and Autumn period in the ancient Chinese State of Lu. His primary historical significance stems from his paternity of Kong Qiu, better known as Confucius, whose philosophical teachings would profoundly shape East Asian civilization for millennia. A scion of the influential Kong clan, Shuliang He navigated the complex political landscape of a fragmented China, where regional states vied for supremacy and internal factions constantly challenged central authority.

As a respected figure within the Lu aristocracy, Shuliang He held administrative posts and earned renown for his martial prowess. Records describe him as a man of exceptional strength and courage, notably during the siege of Bi in 563 BCE. When the city gate was lowered prematurely, trapping Lu forces, Shuliang He reportedly held the massive portcullis aloft with sheer physical power, allowing his besieged comrades to escape. This act of heroism cemented his reputation, reflecting the ideal of the warrior-scholar prevalent among the noble class of the time. He was also instrumental in defending the city of Fang, demonstrating strategic acumen alongside his physical might.

Shuliang He's most enduring legacy, however, is inextricably linked to his son, Confucius. Although he died when Confucius was only three years old, Shuliang He's noble lineage and the social standing he achieved provided the foundational context for Confucius's upbringing and subsequent intellectual development. The values of duty, martial virtue, and public service that characterized Shuliang He's life likely influenced the nascent philosophical inquiries of his son, contributing indirectly to the ethical and political framework that would become Confucianism, a cornerstone of Chinese thought and governance."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:22:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36611350732", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "621 BCE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "621 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing political fragmentation and internecine warfare of the Spring and Autumn period necessitated strong military leaders and administrators like Shuliang He.", "His noble birth within the Kong clan in the State of Lu provided him with opportunities for education and advancement in both civil and military spheres.", "The prevailing cultural ideals of the Zhou dynasty emphasized a blend of scholarly knowledge and martial skill for aristocratic men, which Shuliang He embodied."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-29T18:22:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36611350732", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "547 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "547 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Shuliang He's lineage and social standing provided the family environment into whi